Let X be an infinite-dimensional Banach space (over the real or complex field). Recall that a Banach space Y is reflexive if the canonical isometric embedding of Y into its bidual Y** is surjective, that c₀ denotes the Banach space of real (or complex) sequences converging to 0 equipped with the supremum norm, and that ℓ₁ denotes the Banach space of absolutely summable sequences equipped with the norm ‖(aₙ)‖ = Σ|aₙ|. Two Banach spaces are isomorphic if there is a bounded linear bijection between them whose inverse is also bounded.

Question: Must X contain a closed infinite-dimensional linear subspace Y such that at least one of the following holds?
(i) Y is reflexive;
(ii) Y is isomorphic to c₀;
(iii) Y is isomorphic to ℓ₁.

